{"at":19.87,"posted":{"prompt_id":"p1","permission_mode":"auto","effort":{"level":"medium"},"hook_event_name":"Stop","stop_hook_active":false,"last_assistant_message":"The fox had lived under the Hollis barn for three winters before anyone noticed her. When the boy finally did, he didn't tell his father. He told the hens.\n\n\"There's a fox,\" he whispered at the coop door, as if it were a secret worth keeping. The hens didn't care. They had never cared about anything but grain.\n\nEvery dusk the fox watched the kitchen window, where a woman stood at the sink humming a hymn gone soft at the edges. The fox knew the sounds of that house: the screen door, the rattle of the milk pail, the way the boy dragged his left boot across the porch.\n\nWinter came hard that year. Snow filled the fence line, and the fox's tracks, which had crossed the yard every night for years, vanished by morning. The hens grew thin. The boy grew thinner, though he hid it well, pocketing his supper crusts for the birds. On the coldest nights he stood in the barn doorway in his stocking feet, calling low into the dark, and the fox, who had learned no one's name but his, came a little closer each time.\n\nOne night she caught a rabbit at the edge of the woods, the first she'd caught since autumn. She carried it across the yard, past the barn, up the porch steps, and set it on the mat by the door.\n\nThen she sat, tail wrapped around her paws, and waited.\n\nIn the morning the woman found the rabbit. The boy found the fox, asleep on the step, breathing small clouds into the gray light. He didn't run. He sat down in the snow beside her, close enough to feel her warmth, and they watched the sun climb over the frozen fields.\n\nNeither of them moved until the hens started complaining.","background_tasks":[],"session_crons":[]}}
